%S A398582 1,2,3,4,5,5,6,7,8,9,10,10,11,12,12,13,14,14,15,15,16,17,18,18,19,20,
%T A398582 21,21,22,22,23,24,25,26,26,27,28,29,30,31,32,32,33,34,34,35,36,36,37,
%U A398582 38,39,40,41,41,42,42,43,44,45,45,46,47,48,49,50,50,51,52,53,54
%N A398582 a(n) is the maximum size of a subset S of {1,...,n} such that no two distinct x, y in S have x+y dividing 2*x*y.
%C A398582 This is the second question of Erdős problem #327: Erdős and Graham asked whether such S must satisfy |S| = o(n). Will Sawin (2026) proved the answer is no: there are such S of positive density.
%C A398582 The condition "(x+y) | 2*x*y" says the harmonic mean of x and y is an integer. The variant condition "(x+y) | x*y" (equivalently, 1/x + 1/y is a unit fraction) gives A384927. Every pair forbidden there is forbidden here, so a(n) <= A384927(n); the sequences first differ at a(30) = 22.
%C A398582 Values for n <= 1000 were first computed by Sharvil Kesarwani (see the discussion thread on the Erdős Problems site, Nov 2025); n<=1200 were computed independently obtained by exact integer programming (zero MIP gap).
%H A398582 William Alexander Carney, <a href="/A398582/b398582.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1200</a>
%H A398582 Thomas Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/327">Problem 327</a>, Erdős Problems.
%H A398582 Will Sawin, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.15419">Sets of unit fractions without two members whose average is a unit fraction</a>, arXiv:2607.15419 [math.NT], 2026.
%e A398582 For n = 6 the only forbidden pairs are {2,6} (8 divides 24) and {3,6} (9 divides 36), so removing 6 leaves {1,2,3,4,5} and a(6) = 5.
%Y A398582 Cf. A384927, A390394, A390395.
%K A398582 nonn,new
